            <ArticleTitle>Understanding geographical inequities in diabetes: multilevel evidence from 114,755 adults in Sydney, Australia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e68-73</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.diabres.2014.09.033</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0168-8227(14)00433-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Multilevel analysis revealed the odds of having diabetes varied geographically by 42% among 114,755 persons. Approximately 9% of this variation was attributable to behavioural risk factors, 41% due to health status and obesity, 26% due to socioeconomic circumstances and 13% to country of birth. Contextual risk factors warrant future investigation.</AbstractText>
